Q: Is it possible to implement `Functor&lt;T&gt;` in Java? A: <p>This works reasonably well for me, but it's not quite <code>Functor&lt;T&gt;</code>.  It requires that you also specify <code>F</code>, the type for which your functor is an instance:</p>
<pre><code>interface Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; {
  B apply(A a);
}

interface Functor&lt;A, F&gt; {
  &lt;B&gt; F map(Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Your <code>Event&lt;A&gt;</code> class would implement <code>Functor</code> like this:</p>
<pre><code>public class Event&lt;A&gt; implements Functor&lt;A, Event&lt;?&gt;&gt; {

  public final A value;

  public Event(A _value) {
    value = _value;
  }

  public &lt;B&gt; Event&lt;B&gt; map(Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f) {
    return new Event&lt;B&gt;(f.apply(value));
  }

  public String toString() {
    return &quot;Event&lt;&quot; + value.getClass().getSimpleName() + &quot;&gt;(&quot; + value.toString() + &quot;)&quot;;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you need a functor for a closed-to-extension class (e.g. <code>Observable</code> from rxjava), you can write one that looks a little more like a type class, however it won't be able to conform to any functor-like interface since Java lacks higher-kinded types:</p>
<pre><code>public class ObservableFunctor {
  public static &lt;A,B&gt; Observable&lt;B&gt; map(Observable&lt;A&gt; fa, Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f) {
    return new Observable&lt;B&gt;(f.apply(fa.value));
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here's a runnable example that uses both <code>Event</code> and <code>Observable</code> from above:</p>
<pre><code>public class FunctorDemo {

  interface Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; {
    B apply(A a);
  }

  interface Functor&lt;A, F&gt; {
    &lt;B&gt; F map(Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f);
  }

  static class ObservableFunctor {
    public static &lt;A,B&gt; Observable&lt;B&gt; map(Observable&lt;A&gt; fa, Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f) {
      return new Observable&lt;B&gt;(f.apply(fa.value));
    }
  }

  static class Observable&lt;A&gt;  {

    public final A value;

    public Observable(A _value) {
      value = _value;
    }

    public String toString() {
      return &quot;Observable&lt;&quot; + value.getClass().getSimpleName() + &quot;&gt;(&quot; + value.toString() + &quot;)&quot;;
    }
  }

  static class Event&lt;A&gt; implements Functor&lt;A, Event&lt;?&gt;&gt; {

    public final A value;

    public Event(A _value) {
      value = _value;
    }

    public &lt;B&gt; Event&lt;B&gt; map(Fn1&lt;A, B&gt; f) {
      return new Event&lt;B&gt;(f.apply(value));
    }

    public String toString() {
      return &quot;Event&lt;&quot; + value.getClass().getSimpleName() + &quot;&gt;(&quot; + value.toString() + &quot;)&quot;;
    }
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {

    Observable&lt;Event&lt;Long&gt;&gt; oe1 = new Observable(new Event(42L));
    System.out.println(&quot;oe1: &quot; + oe1.toString()); // oe1: Observable&lt;Event&gt;(Event&lt;Long&gt;(42))

    Observable&lt;Event&lt;String&gt;&gt; oe2 = ObservableFunctor.map(oe1,
      new Fn1&lt;Event&lt;Long&gt;, Event&lt;String&gt;&gt;() {
        public Event&lt;String&gt; apply(Event&lt;Long&gt; e) {
          return e.map(
            new Fn1&lt;Long, String&gt;() {
              public String apply(Long l) {
                return l.toString();
              }
            }
          );
        }
      }
    );
    System.out.println(&quot;oe2: &quot; + oe2.toString()); // oe2: Observable&lt;Event&gt;(Event&lt;String&gt;(42))

  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to exclude Pydantic fields from deserialisation? A: <p>I found one reasonable option - use a custom validator that always fails:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from typing import Annotated, Any
from pydantic import BaseModel, BeforeValidator, Field

def do_not_deserialize(value: Any) -&gt; Any:
    raise ValueError(&quot;Deserialization is not allowed for this field.&quot;)

class Item(BaseModel):
    name: str
    value: float
    info: Annotated[str, BeforeValidator(do_not_deserialize)] = &quot;default&quot;

def main():
    item_dict = {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Sample Item&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: 10.5}#, &quot;info&quot;: &quot;extra info&quot;}
    item = Item.model_validate(item_dict)
    print(item)

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    main()
</code></pre>
<p>Uncomment the comment and it will throw an error.</p>

Q: How do I detect dark mode using JavaScript? A: <p>To detect dark mode in JavaScript, you can query the user’s preferred color scheme via the CSS media feature <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@media/prefers-color-scheme" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>prefers-color-scheme</code></a>, and access it from JS using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/matchMedia" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>window.matchMedia()</code></a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const darkModeMql = window.matchMedia &amp;&amp; window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');

if (darkModeMql &amp;&amp; darkModeMql.matches) {
  // dark mode
} else {
  // light mode
}
</code></pre>
<p>If the user changes their OS/browser theme while your app is running, you can subscribe to changes by listening to the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MediaQueryList/change_event" rel="nofollow noreferrer">change event</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', event =&gt; {
    const newColorScheme = event.matches ? &quot;dark&quot; : &quot;light&quot;;
    // use newColorScheme
});
</code></pre>
